#13 13.61 This installation shell script will examine your system and ask you questions
#13 13.61 to determine how the perl5 package should be installed. If you get
#13 13.61 stuck on a question, you may use a ! shell escape to start a subshell or
#13 13.61 execute a command.  Many of the questions will have default answers in square
#13 13.61 brackets; typing carriage return will give you the default.
#13 13.61 
#13 13.61 On some of the questions which ask for file or directory names you are allowed
#13 13.61 to use the ~name construct to specify the login directory belonging to "name",
#13 13.61 even if you don't have a shell which knows about that.  Questions where this is
#13 13.61 allowed will be marked "(~name ok)".
#13 13.61 
#13 13.61 [Type carriage return to continue]  
#13 13.62 
#13 13.62 The prompter used in this script allows you to use shell variables and
#13 13.62 backticks in your answers.  You may use $1, $2, etc...  to refer to the words
#13 13.62 in the default answer, as if the default line was a set of arguments given to a
#13 13.62 script shell.  This means you may also use $* to repeat the whole default line,
#13 13.62 so you do not have to re-type everything to add something to the default.
#13 13.62 
#13 13.62 Every time there is a substitution, you will have to confirm.  If there is an
#13 13.62 error (e.g. an unmatched backtick), the default answer will remain unchanged
#13 13.62 and you will be prompted again.
#13 13.62 
#13 13.62 If you are in a hurry, you may run 'Configure -d'.  This will bypass nearly all
#13 13.62 the questions and use the computed defaults (or the previous answers if there
#13 13.62 was already a config.sh file). Type 'Configure -h' for a list of options.
#13 13.62 You may also start interactively and then answer '& -d' at any prompt to turn
#13 13.62 on the non-interactive behaviour for the remainder of the execution.
#13 13.62 
#13 13.62 [Type carriage return to continue]  
